{"id": "7bb0159a-eb83-48eb-a2a5-dba53ce7c977", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T12:14:51.407701", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "00cae002-9bd7-49b2-80a4-3b771cb2f843", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "# Initial Inspection Report and Proposed Research Design (plan stage)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Repository and Material Inspection\n\n### 1.1 `/workspace` contents\n- Single file present: `/workspace/task.json` (637 bytes).\n- The file contains only the JSON envelope with three fields:\n  - `question`: the research question on combined jailbreak techniques (AutoDAN & PAIR), ASR, and interaction dynamics.\n  - `context`: states that this is a *design-only* task and that repositories and compute are not required.\n  - `documents`: references a `track.md` that should give per-topic setup details.\n- No `track.md` file is present in `/workspace` despite being referenced in `documents`. A recursive `find` over `/workspace` returns exactly one file (`task.json`).\n\n### 1.2 Project-related code or documents\n- **None locally.** No code, no PDF, no README, no references file, no auxiliary directory.\n- The metadata field `researcher_topics = [\"neurips2024/97431/11:setup_17\", \"neurips2024/97431/11:setup_18\"]` suggests that the detailed setup strings for this design live under a NeurIPS-2024 paper 97431 and are reachable only by the researcher, not by me. I therefore cannot read them and have not assumed their contents.\n- The runtime bubblewrap environment hints: `track = design-only`, `network = disabled`, `gpu_enabled = false`, `cpu_affinity_count = 24`, Python 3.12.3 system interpreter, no project dependencies preinstalled, ~125 GB RAM, ~720 GB free disk. This is consistent with a design-only task, but I want the researcher to confirm whether any book-keeping (figures, tables of expected ASR ranges) is wanted.\n\n### 1.3 Missing items list (what I do *not* have and need from the researcher)\n1. The actual text of `track.md` and the `setup_17` / `setup_18` topic strings — they define the legally-ratified experimental setup.\n2. Specification of which base LLM family is the *victim* (e.g., Vicuna-7B/13B, Llama-2-Chat-7B/13B, Mistral-7B-Instruct, or another).\n3. Specification of the *attack models* for PAIR (attacker LLM and judge LLM) and the *surrogate/fitness model* for AutoDAN.\n4. Concrete enumeration of \"previously tested tricks\" (the question names this factor but the source materials do not enumerate it).\n5. Concrete defense mechanisms and their strength tiers.\n6. Datasets to run (AdvBench, MaliciousInstruct, JailbreakBench, others?).\n7. Evaluation protocol: which ASR definition (substring match vs. LLM-judge vs. human), attack budget (number of queries, generations, crossover/selection rounds), and number of random seeds.\n8. Compute / wall-clock budget, if any.\n9. Whether the researcher wants response style: mock tables, expected direction of effects, or only the design skeleton.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Proposed Research Design (high-level draft)\n\n### 2.1 Refined research questions / hypotheses\n- **RQ1 (main effect):** How does attacker strength, defense strength, and the number of previously tested tricks independently affect ASR under each attack style (AutoDAN, PAIR)?\n- **RQ2 (interaction):** Does the marginal benefit of a stronger attacker diminish as defense strength grows (or vice-versa)? Does attack style moderate this trade-off?\n- **RQ3 (transfer):** Do effects observed on one dataset generalize to another (in particular, between AdvBench-style \"Harmful Behaviors\" and JailbreakBench behaviors)?\n\nSketch hypotheses (labelled as *inferred*, awaiting researcher confirmation):\n- H1: Attacker strength ↑ ⇒ ASR ↑; Defense strength ↑ ⇒ ASR ↓.\n- H2: A×D sub-additive at high defense (defense saturates gains from weak attackers; strong attackers partially bypass).\n- H3: Tricks affect ASR asymmetrically — token-level (AutoDAN) gains more from token-aware tricks (suffix length, encoding) while prompt-level (PAIR) gains more from semantic tricks (persona, refusal suppression).\n- H4: Stronger defense is more robust to prompt-style attacks than to token-style attacks (AutoDAN suffixes exploit the LM directly and may evade semantic defenses like SmoothLLM paraphrasing).\n\n### 2.2 Variables and factors\n| Role | Variable | Type | Levels (proposed) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Independent | Attack style `S` | nominal (2) | {AutoDAN, PAIR} |\n| Independent | Attacker strength `A` | ordinal (3) | weak / medium / strong (operationalizations in §2.3) |\n| Independent | Defense strength `D` | ordinal (4) | none / mild / moderate / strong |\n| Independent | Previously tested tricks `T` | ordinal (4) | 0 / 1 / 2 / 3 categories of trick activated |\n| Random / blocking | Dataset `Ds` | nominal (2–3) | e.g., AdvBench + JailbreakBench (+ optional MaliciousInstruct) |\n| Random | Prompt `p` | random sample | n per dataset (see §2.5) |\n| Random | Seed `r` | random | r ∈ {1,…,5} |\n| Dependent | ASR | bounded continuous / binary per prompt | ASR over n prompts per cell |\n\n### 2.3 Operationalization of factors (label as *inferred* unless ratified)\n- **Attacker strength `A`** (paper-agnostic defaults; needs researcher sign-off):\n  - weak = small open model, low jailbreak expertise, narrow sampling (A1)\n  - medium = mid-size chat model, few-shot jailbreak exemplars, moderate sampling (A2)\n  - strong = large chat model + curated jailbreak system prompt, broader candidate pool (A3)\n  - Needs to be paired per attack style: in PAIR, attacker LLM strength; in AutoDAN, GA population size, mutation rate, and the rank/size of the surrogate sentence encoder modeling diversity.\n- **Defense strength `D`** (defenses must apply to both attack styles for fair comparison):\n  - D0: no defense\n  - D1: system-prompt hardening only (\"You must refuse harmful requests, here are examples…\")\n  - D2: D1 + perplexity-based input filter at threshold τ≈mean+2σ\n  - D3: D2 + output-side smoothing/paraphrase or self-check\n- **Previously tested tricks `T`** (this factor is the most underspecified by the source):\n  - T0: base scaffold only (AutoDAN genetic suffix on the harmful prompt; PAIR attacker given only the goal).\n  - T1: + Persona/roleplay wrapper (e.g., \"DAN\", \"developer mode\").\n  - T2: T1 + refusal-suppression / success-framing (\"Begin your reply with 'Sure, here is…'\").\n  - T3: T2 + obfuscation: base64 / Unicode homoglyph / payload-splitting / payload-as-code targeting token-level defenses in AutoDAN.\n  - For the defense side, \"previously tested\" can also be operationalized as whether the defense's filter lexicon has signatures from category k (k=1,2,3); define T_clean = unseen-by-filter, T_seen = same trick family was used to calibrate the filter.\n- **Prompts/behaviors `p`**: held fixed across A, D, T cells so that ASR differences reflect factor effects, not prompt difficulty. Counterbalanced ordering of trials to avoid carryover.\n\n### 2.4 Comparison hierarchy (planned contrasts)\n- Within each attack style S, full factorial over A × D × T (3×4×4 = 48 cells / style, 96 cells total).\n- For each cell, evaluate ASR on each dataset: 96 × (2–3) cells.\n- For each cell, repeat over r=5 random seeds (initial populations for AutoDAN; attacker temperature and seed pool for PAIR).\n- Planned contrasts:\n  - Main effects: marginal-mean ASR per level of A, D, T, separately per S.\n  - Two-way: A×D, A×T, D×T, A×S, D×S, T×S.\n  - Three-way: A×D×S (the headline interaction the question asks about), A×T×S, D×T×S.\n- Generalizability: replicate every cell on a second dataset; treat `Ds` as a blocking factor and report effects both *within* and *across* datasets.\n\n### 2.5 Datasets and model families (inferred, awaiting ratification)\n- **Datasets** (choose two for within-experiment replication, third optional):\n  - AdvBench \"Harmful Behaviors\" (≈520 behaviors; use a fixed 100 sub-sample to bound budget).\n  - JailbreakBench behaviors (100; canonically paired with PAIR evaluation).\n  - MaliciousInstruct (100; optional third dataset).\n- **Target models (victim)**: at minimum two — instruct-tuned chat model from one family, e.g., Vicuna-7B-v1.5 and Llama-2-Chat-7B. Architecture should match what the researcher has licensed/prepared.\n- **Attacker model for PAIR**: small-to-large gradient, e.g., Vicuna-13B (weak), Mistral-7B-Instruct (medium), Llama-2-Chat-13B or Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct (strong). Same attacker family must be applied across A levels for fair comparison.\n- **Judge model for PAIR** (the scorer that decides whether the attack succeeded): same family across cells, fixed at one designation (e.g., the medium-tier attacker) or use a stronger one to reduce grading noise. Document.\n- **Surrogate/fitness for AutoDAN**: a sentence-similarity encoder (e.g., a Sentence-BERT variant) that remains fixed within the experiment.\n\n### 2.6 Sample size and execution scale\n- Per cell: n=100 prompts × r=5 seeds = 500 scored attempts.\n- Per style (AutoDAN or PAIR): 48 cells × 100 prompts × 5 seeds = 24,000 attempts.\n- Total (both styles, two datasets): 96 × 100 × 5 × 2 = **96,000 attempts**; with three datasets, **144,000**.\n- The two \"paired\" datasets mostly serve as replication, so as a budget-saver we could run all cells on Dataset 1 and 25-cell subsample on Dataset 2 — the researcher can choose.\n- Statistical power: with binary outcome (success/not) at n=100 prompts and 5 seeds per cell, marginal-mean ASR differences of ~10 pp between adjacent factor levels are detectable with high power (>0.85) at α=0.05 in a two-proportion test; smaller effects (3–5 pp) need both datasets pooled.\n\n### 2.7 Analysis plan — main effects and interactions\n- **Primary model**: multilevel logistic regression on per-prompt per-seed success.\n  `logit(P(success)) = β0 + β_A(A) + β_D(D) + β_T(T) + β_S(S) + β_A×D(A·D) + β_A×T(A·T) + β_D×T(D·T) + β_A×S(A·S) + β_D×S(D·S) + β_T×S(T·S) + β_A×D×S(A·D·S) + β_A×T×S(A·T·S) + β_D×T×S(D·T·S) + u_prompt + u_seed`. Ordinally-coded factors can be tested for linear vs. nonlinear trend.\n- For pairwise interactions that are the question's headline (A×D, A×D×S), fit reduced nested models and use likelihood-ratio tests.\n- **Effect-size reporting**: odds ratios, marginal-effects plots showing ASR as a function of A at each level of D, separately per S; interaction plots for A×D×S.\n- **Robustness checks**:\n  - Replace LLM judge with substring-match ASR and check rank stability of effects.\n  - Drop highest-leverage prompts (very long / very short) and rerun.\n  - Bootstrap over `r` seeds to get CI on marginal ASR.\n- **Dataset generalization**: include `Ds` as fixed factor and report by-dataset marginal effects plus the A×D×S×Ds four-way to test transferability.\n- **Pre-registration-ish artefacts**: register the factor definitions, level counts, n prompts per cell, evaluation metrics, and exclusion rules *before* any data is collected (this is design-only, so we capture them in the design document).\n\n### 2.8 Feasibility / sanity checks visible from the repo\n- None of the code, weights, or datasets are present. So this design cannot be *executed* in `/workspace` — it is deliverable as a design document only, consistent with the task.json context.\n- If the researcher wants a lightweight feasibility check (e.g., a tiny dry-run with the system Meta-Llama tokenizer / a small embedding model surrogate), it can be drafted if/when network or weights become available; I will not assume so now.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Key Assumptions and Uncertainties\n\n### 3.1 Fixed by the task context (not negotiable)\n1. **Design-only deliverable.** No experiment will be claimed as run, per `task.json` and the environment note.\n2. **Two attack styles: AutoDAN (token-level) and PAIR (prompt-level).** Encoded by the question wording.\n3. **Outcome variable: ASR.** Encoded by the question.\n4. **Three independent dimensions** of \"combined jailbreak techniques\": **attacker strength, defense strength, previously tested tricks.** Encoded by the question wording.\n5. **The runtime is bubblewrap with no network and no preinstalled project dependencies.** So nothing can be fetched or run end-to-end during the task.\n\n### 3.2 Open assumptions that the source materials do *not* spell out (must ask the researcher)\nThese are my *inferred* defaults — they are *not* hard requirements of the task. Each is a question that should be resolved before finalizing the experiment design:\n\nA1. **Operationalization of \"attacker strength\".** I assumed a 3-tier ladder of attacker LLMs for PAIR and a 3-tier ladder of AutoDAN GA-budget/surrogate quality. The actual paper (setup_17) may operationalize strength by *query budget* or by *population size* or by *mutation operators*. Needs confirmation.\nA2. **Operationalization of \"defense strength\".** I assumed a four-tier ladder from none to output-side smoothing/self-check. The original paper might use specific named defenses (Perplexity Filter, SmoothLLM, Self-Reminder, RAIN, etc.). Needs confirmation.\nA3. **Operationalization of \"previously tested tricks\".** This is the most under-defined factor. My interpretation is a {0,1,2,3}-trick ladder combining persona, refusal suppression, encoding/obfuscation, and payload-splitting. An alternative is \"seen-vs-unseen-by-defense-filter\" or \"warm-start vs cold-start\" of the attack scaffolding. Needs confirmation.\nA4. **Victim model family.** Defaults to Vicuna-7B and Llama-2-Chat-7B. Original paper may use a different pair (e.g., Llama-2-7B-Chat + GPT-3.5-as-target). Needs confirmation.\nA5. **Attacker / judge model family for PAIR.** Defaults to a small-to-large gradient within the same family. Needs confirmation.\nA6. **Surrogate/fitness model for AutoDAN.** I assumed a Sentence-BERT variant with fixed embedding dimension. Needs confirmation.\nA7. **Dataset choice and subset size.** I assumed AdvBench (subset of 100) and JailbreakBench (100), with optional MaliciousInstruct. Needs confirmation.\nA8. **ASR definition (judge).** I assumed LLM-judge (PAIR's native protocol) plus a substring-match fallback for robustness. The original protocol might require human-rated ASR; that would change budget dramatically. Needs confirmation.\nA9. **Random seed count and budget.** I assumed r=5 seeds per cell; the original may fix a single seed or 3. Needs confirmation.\nA10. **Query budget per attack.** PAIR is typically a 20-step conversation; AutoDAN is a 100-generation GA. I assumed those defaults; needs confirmation, since these budgets *are* part of the operationalization of attacker strength.\nA11. **Which of the dimensions are *controlled within cell* versus *random across cells*.** I assumed: prompt fixed across cells of a dataset; dataset fixed per replication arm; seed randomized within cell. Needs confirmation.\nA12. **Reporting unit.** Per-prompt, then averaged per cell; or per-conversation aggregated; or unique-jailbreak found per N attempts. Needs confirmation.\n\n### 3.3 Empirical-direction assumptions (hypotheses I am taking as starting priors — not facts)\n- ASR increases monotonically with attacker strength and decreases monotonically with defense strength. (Reasonable but not guaranteed.)\n- \"Stronger defense\" is more effective against prompt-style attacks than against token-level mutations because token mutations live below the semantic layer the defense inspects. (Inferred, awaiting source.)\n- Tricks have a positive main effect on ASR, but the dominant mechanism differs by attack style. (Inferred.)\n- Interaction A×D is sub-additive at high defense — strong defenses cap the marginal gain of a stronger attacker. (Inferred, may not hold for certain defenses.)\n\n### 3.4 Uncertainties that could change the design materially\n1. Whether the original paper treats `T` (tricks) as categorical, ordinal, or as \"exposure to defender filter\" — this changes coding and statistical model.\n2. Whether AutoDAN and PAIR are run against the *same* target / same defense or whether each attack style is paired with its native defense paradigm — the cross-style comparison becomes much weaker in the latter case.\n3. Whether the \"tricks\" dimension is attack-side, defense-side, or both. My draft assumes attack-side (the attacker dresses the prompt with tricks); a defense-side reading would change the design.\n4. Whether the question's \"previously tested tricks\" is meant to include *warm-starting* (transfer of attack scaffolding across prompts/seeds), which would introduce a *within-cell sequence* dimension not currently in my design.\n5. Whether the researcher expects the design to include a *human evaluation* layer for ASR — that has cost order-of-magnitude higher.\n\n### 3.5 Things I have *not* assumed\n- I have *not* assumed that any concrete ASR number or p-value is the expected outcome.\n- I have *not* assumed model weights are downloadable; the design is model-class-only.\n- I have *not* assumed the contents of the NeurIPS-2024 paper 97431 text.\n\n---\n\n## Outstanding clarifications I will need before producing the final (result-stage) design\n\n1. Confirm or replace my operationalization of `A`, `D`, and `T` (most critical).\n2. Confirm victim / attacker / judge / surrogate model identities and sizes.\n3. Confirm the dataset list and prompt sample size per cell.\n4. Confirm the ASR evaluation protocol (LLM-judge vs substring match vs human).\n5. Confirm whether \"tricks\" is attack-side, defense-side, or both.\n6. Confirm whether AutoDAN and PAIR should be evaluated against identical defenses, or whether each gets its native defense paradigm; this decision drives the cross-style interaction term.\n7. Confirm the random-seed count, query budget per attack, and any compute/wall-clock constraint.\n\nI will treat items A1–A12 above as **draft assumptions subject to your revision**, not as fixed requirements.", "evidence": "## Repository inspection evidence\n- `ls -la /workspace` → only `task.json` (637 bytes) and