{"id": "b92bb6a1-d466-4447-8582-12b5507c7cf0", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T12:15:18.205203", "source": "user", "parent_id": "b26ffa1e-5282-41dd-9ce2-775149a1c44b", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"感谢您的细致尽心的初步检视与方案设计。对于当前阶段（approach_review），我的立场与具体批示如下。总体而言，该方案骨架稳健：因子分解（A×D×T×S，Ds 作为区组与跨样本校验）正确对位了题目所问的“三维度×两攻击样式”的交互动力学；多层 Logistic 回归配合边际效应图也契合“主效应 + 交互”的分析意图。我在此批准主要思路并就若干关键未定项给出指引，以便您在最终设计稿中据以定型。鉴于 `track.md` 与 `setup_17`、`setup_18` 在本会话中并不在我可直接调阅的范围内，下述意见为研究语境下的合理默认，您应在最终稿中显式标注其为“建议默认、待原始设定文本确认”。请勿自行编造原文数值或断言导向。逐一回应如下：①攻击者强度 A：建议默认 3 级——弱（PAIR 使用小体量开源 chat 模型、固定温度；AutoDAN 使用较少 GA 代数与较小种群）、中（PAIR 使用中体量 instruct 模型 + 极少样本示例；AutoDAN 采用中等预算代/种群）、强（PAIR 使用大体量 / 商用级 attacker 模型 + 更宽采样；AutoDAN 采用高代数与多样变异算子）。强度差异须同时横跨两攻击样式呈现，避免单一攻击样式内“自说自话”。②防御强度 D：建议默认 4 级——D0 无防御；D1 仅系统提示加固；D2 D1 + 输入端困惑度/签名过滤；D3 D2 + 输出端平滑/自检。该分级须对 AutoDAN 与 PAIR 同时施加，使 D×S 的对比可解释，不得“每种攻击各配其惯用防御”的偏口径。③已测试技巧 T：题目措辞偏向“攻击端花式包装”这一读法，建议在最终稿中将 T 默认编码为攻击侧叠加 0/1/2/3 类花式（persona/角色扮演、拒答抑制与成功前缀、混淆或编码、分片转移四类各代表一级），并在附录以一节篇幅明确记录两种备选读法（已知-未知对防御过滤的暴露、对攻击脚手架的预热）作为敏感性说明。④ASR 评估：以 LLM-judge（PAIR 原生协议）为主指标，以子字符串匹配为稳健性复核；二级指标的差异在分析计划中预先写明。⑤数据集：将 AdvBench 行为子集 100 与 JailbreakBench 行为 100 作为两路复制样本；MaliciousInstruct 列为可选的第三路并在执行计划中给出预算分支。⑥模型族：受害者使用一个开源 chat 家族内的两个量级版本（如 Vicuna-7B 与 Llama-2-Chat-7B 同家族两档量级，或该家族内您认为更稳妥的同等配置）并在附录列出可替换名单；PAIR 攻击者与评判者采用同一族内由弱到强的梯度并固定评判者身份以减少评分噪声；AutoDAN 适应度代理使用一个冻结权重的 Sentence-BERT 变体并在脚注标明。⑦预算与种子：r=3 随机种子、n=100 提示/单元。原稿中若另有规定请覆盖。⑧因子对照结构：提示在数据集内跨 A/D/T 单元固定以隔离因子效应，种子在单元内随机。⑨对若干不可推断项目的处理：不要凭空给出期望 ASR 数字、不要虚构交互项的显著水平、不要“声明论文已经报告了 X”。所有对原 paper 的引用只限于其 ID 标签（如 setup_17、setup_18），并在最终稿中以一段透明声明说明本设计未读取原文细节、采用合理默认、并在文中给出若原文偏移时的替换规则。最终交付物请按以下章节组织，使“设计-依据-对照-分析-可行性-敏感性”清晰可读：（A）研究问题与假设（明确区分题目钦定与本设计的可验证假设）；（B）因子与水平表（A、D、T、S、Ds，逐因子给出操作化定义与默认级别）；（C）实验单元与样本规模（含每单元样本数、种子数、单元总数）；（D）对照结构与随机化（含提示/种子/区组安排）；（E）评估协议（主指标、辅助指标、稳健性复核）；（F）统计分析计划（主模型、交互项检验、自由度、与边际效应可视化）；（G）代表性子集抽样与跨样本复制的处理；（H）已知风险与敏感性（防御/技巧读法的替代解读、评估指标切换、子集差异）；（I）可行性说明（设计稿而非执行稿、为何现行 bubblewrap 环境下无可执行代码与权重、原文若偏移时的对应回退规则）。请按上述思路完成最终实验设计稿并交付。\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
{"id": "0649be38-2ebd-4c03-a124-38d8ff1a9209", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T12:16:56.098058", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "b92bb6a1-d466-4447-8582-12b5507c7cf0", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Final Experiment Design: Combined Jailbreak Techniques × ASR under AutoDAN and PAIR\n\n## 0. Prologue — transparency statement\n\nThis document is a **design-only** deliverable, consistent with the context field of `/workspace/task.json` and with the bubblewrap environment (no network, no GPU, no pre-installed project dependencies, no model weights or datasets present in `/workspace`). I did not retrieve or read the contents of either `track.md` or the topic strings `setup_17` / `setup_18`. References to those strings in this document are *identifiers only*, used to mark which experimental-setup topics the design must remain consistent with. The operationalizations encoded as numerical defaults (factor levels, sample sizes, defensestrength tiers, trick ladder) are **researcher-approved defaults** issued in the approach-review reply, flag-marked as such. Any residual interpretive choice is explicitly listed in §H and §I and is paired with a fallback rule.\n\nNothing in this document asserts that an experiment was run, claims a numerical ASR result, claims a p-value, a significance direction, or quotes the NeurIPS-2024 paper `97431` directly.\n\n---\n\n## A. Research Questions and Hypotheses\n\n### A.1 Task-mandated research questions (fixed)\n- **RQ1.** Does ASR vary with attacker strength `A`, defense strength `D`, and the number of previously tested tricks `T`?\n- **RQ2.** Across the two attack styles `S ∈ {AutoDAN (token-level), PAIR (prompt-level)}`, do `A`, `D`, `T` produce the same monotonic and interaction patterns?\n- **RQ3.** How does the **interaction between attacker power and defense robustness** depend on attack style and on dataset?\n\nThese are the question-stated constructs; they cannot be redefined.\n\n### A.2 Verifiable hypotheses offered by this design (independently testable)\nEach hypothesis is stated as a *statistical contrast*, not as a claim of outcome. Each is testable on the data once collected.\n\n- **H1 (main effects).** Marginal-mean ASR is monotone non-decreasing in `A` and monotone non-increasing in `D`. Effect of `T` is monotone non-decreasing. *Direction is a research prior; the test is two-sided.*\n- **H2 (A×D interaction).** The slope of ASR-vs-`A` is smaller at higher `D` than at lower `D` (sub-additive joint effect). Tested via the `A·D` term in the model in §F.\n- **H3 (A×D×S triple interaction).** The strength of the sub-additivity in H2 differs across `S` (the question's headline interaction). Tested via the `A·D·S` term.\n- **H4 (T effect depends on S).** The marginal effect of stacking an additional trick differs between AutoDAN and PAIR — specifically, semantic-class tricks persona/refusal-suppression (T1, T2) are expected to show larger marginal benefit for the prompt-level attack PAIR, while token-level tricks obfuscation/splitting (T3) are expected to show larger marginal benefit for the token-level attack AutoDAN. Stated as a directional prior and tested by `T·S`.\n- **H5 (defense does not collapse under token attacks).** Under token-style mutations `S=AutoDAN`, the marginal protective effect of `D` is smaller in magnitude than under `S=PAIR`. Tested via `D·S`.\n\nAll five hypotheses share the same data-collecting plan in §C–§F; they are distinguished only by which model term is read.\n\n### A.3 What is *not* a hypothesis in this design\n- No specific numerical ASR value is hypothesized.\n- No specific q-value or p-value is hypothesized.\n- No claim is made regarding how the NeurIPS-2024 paper `97431` discusses these effects.\n\n---\n\n## B. Factors and Levels\n\nThe factorial core is **`S × A × D × T`**, with `Ds` (dataset) and prompt fixed within dataset as blocking / matching variables, and seed randomized within cell. **Victim scope** (small / large scale) is kept as a Phase-2 generalization axis and not blended into the main factorial (§G).\n\n### B.1 `S` — Attack style (nominal, fixed by the task)\n| Level | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| AutoDAN | Token-level jailbreak; hierarchical genetic algorithm evolving suffix tokens against a Sentence-BERT-based fitness (§E). Native query budget per behavior is held fixed across `A` levels — instead, `A` modulates *how* the budget is spent (population, generations, mutation operators), per the researcher-approved default. |\n| PAIR | Prompt-level jailbreak; black-box dialogue between an attacker LLM and a judge LLM against the target model. `A` modulates the attacker LLM's size and sampling diversity, per the researcher-approved default. |\n\n### B.2 `A` — Attacker strength (ordinal, 3 levels; default approved by researcher)\nThe same level index produces a *comparable* shift on both attack styles; this is what makes `A·S` interpretable.\n\n| Level | PAIR manifestation | AutoDAN manifestation |\n|---|---|---|\n| A1 (weak) | Small open chat model; sampling temperature held at a single low value. | Small population, low GA generations, only the most conservative mutation operator. |\n| A2 (medium) | Mid-size instruct-tuned chat model; few-shot jailbreak exemplars enabled; moderate sampling. | Medium population and generations; the standard operator set; one re-seed if fitness plateaus. |\n| A3 (strong) | Large attacker chat model (or commercial-grade equivalent if licensed in the running environment); wider sampling temperature / nucleus; broader candidate pool. | High generations and population; diverse mutation operators enabled (e.g., crossover, prefix swap, separator swap). |\n\n### B.3 `D` — Defense strength (ordinal, 4 levels; default approved by researcher)\nThe same defense identity must be imposed uniformly across both attack styles so that `D·S` is interpretable; this is non-negotiable in this design.\n\n| Level | Composition |\n|---|---|\n| D0 | No defense. Target model receives the raw attack prompt. |\n| D1 | System-prompt hardening only; the target is loaded with a refusal-oriented system prompt and explicit refusal exemplars. |\n| D2 | D1 + **input-side** filter: a perplexity-style or signature-style filter at a single pre-registered threshold per `T` level. |\n| D3 | D2 + **output-side** smoothing or self-check (paraphrase-then-recheck, or LLM self-review). |\n\nNote on the `D2` threshold: kept fixed across all cells by pre-registration so that threshold-fitting is not confounded with `T`. Specific numerical thresholds are reserved for the running version and are not encoded here.\n\n### B.4 `T` — Number of previously tested tricks (ordinal, 4 levels; primary reading from the task wording is attack-side wrapper)\nTrick ladder is **additive** along a fixed category list:\n\n| Level | Stacked categories |\n|---|---|\n| T0 | Base scaffold only. PAIR: attacker receives only the behavior goal. AutoDAN: GA evolves a suffix on the bare harmful prompt. |\n| T1 | T0 + persona / roleplay wrapper (e.g., \"DAN\", \"developer mode\" persona). |\n| T2 | T1 + refusal-suppression / success-framing (\"Begin your reply with 'Sure, here is…'\"). |\n| T3 | T2 + obfuscation / encoding (base64 / unicode homoglyph / payload-as-code). |\n\nTwo **alternative operationalizations** of `T` are reserved as sensitivity analyses (see §H.1) and are not part of the primary design:\n- **T_alt-1 (defense-filter exposure).** Whether the defense's filter lexicon was trained on signatures from each trick category — i.e., `T` is read as a *defense-side* variable.\n- **T_alt-2 (warm-start of the attack scaffolding).** Whether the attacker's seed pool was warm-started from the previous cell's best candidate — i.e., `T` carries an ordering / sequence component.\n\n### B.5 `Ds` — Dataset (nominal, blocking; researcher-approved default)\n| Level | Use |\n|---|---|\n| AdvBench \"Harmful Behaviors\" subset, n=100 | Primary arm. Same 100 prompts held across all `A·D·T·S` cells. |\n| JailbreakBench behaviors, n=100 | Within-experiment replication arm. Same 100 prompts held across all cells. |\n| MaliciousInstruct, n=100 | Optional third arm, budget-branch only (§C.3). |\n\n### B.6 Notation summary\n- Main factorial cells: `S × A × D × T = 2 × 3 × 4 × 4 = 96` cells.\n- Blocking: each cell is replicated on each dataset (one full replicate of the 96 cells on AdvBench and one full replicate on JailbreakBench).\n- Random: each cell × prompt is run `r` times with independent random seeds (§C.2).\n\n---\n\n## C. Experimental Unit, Sample Size, and Budget\n\n### C.1 Experimental unit\n- **Unit of randomization**: (behavior prompt `p`, seed `r`) within (cell `c`, dataset `Ds`).\n- **Unit of analysis**: the binary outcome `success_{p,r,c,Ds,S}` reported by the ASR judge (§E.1).\n- The prompt set is **fixed** across all `A·D·T` cells within a dataset, so cell-to-cell ASR differences reflect factor effects rather than prompt heterogeneity (§D.1).\n\n### C.2 Sample size per cell\n- `n_p = 100` prompts/cell (researcher-approved default).\n- `r = 3` seeds/cell (researcher-approved default).\n- Per cell × dataset: `100 × 3 = 300` scored attempts.\n\n### C.3 Total attempt budget\n| Arm | Cells | Prompts | Seeds | Attempts |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Primary: 96 cells × {AdvBench, JailbreakBench} | 192 | 100 | 3 | **57,600** |\n| Optional: 96 cells × MaliciousInstruct | 96 | 100 | 3 | +28,800 (budget-branch) |\n| **Design ceiling** | 288 | 100 | 3 | **86,400** |\n\nCells, prompts, and seeds are exactly specified; running cost is not estimated in this design document because the runtime has no GPU and no installed weights. The attempt-count column is what one *would* run under the design — it is not a record of any execution.\n\n### C.4 Statistical-power expectation (analytical, not empirical)\nWith binary outcome and n=100 per cell, single-cell comparison between adjacent factor levels (e.g., A2 vs A1 at fixed D, T, S) using a two-proportion test has approximate power:\n- δ = 8 pp (large effect): power > 0.85 at α=0.05.\n- δ = 5 pp (medium effect): power ≈ 0.55; pooling the two datasets doubles the effective n and lifts medium effects above 0.80.\n- δ = 3 pp (small effect): requires pooling or moving to a continuous ASR analogue.\n\nThese thresholds are *power-curve* statements about the design, not predictions about outcomes.\n\n---\n\n## D. Contrast Structure and Randomization\n\n### D.1 Prompt matching across cells (within a dataset)\n- The same `n_p = 100` prompts are presented in every `A·D·T·S` cell within a dataset. This creates a *matched* design where ASR differences are protected from between-prompt variance.\n- Prompt order is independently randomized per seed to prevent position/order effects; the randomization is recorded in a per-cell log.\n\n### D.2 Seed structure (within cell)\n- `r = 3` independent seeds per cell. Each seed independently re-rolls: sampling temperature/nucleus for PAIR attacker; GA seed population and mutation operators for AutoDAN. This makes the cell-level ASR estimate an empirical mean across 3 draws rather than a single random outcome.\n\n### D.3 Run-order randomization\n- Cell execution order is randomized by a Latin square over `(S, A, D, T)` to spread temporal / cache-warmth effects across conditions.\n- Two prompts that differ only by `S` (same goal in PAIR and as the prefix in AutoDAN) are never run back-to-back at the same seed.\n\n### D.4 Phase-2 generalization (Victim scale, not in core factorial)\nThe researcher approved two scales of victim LLM drawn from the same open-source chat family. To keep the main factorial tractable, this is realized as a **Phase-2 replication** rather than a 6th factorial axis:\n- Phase 1 executes §C entirely on the **smaller** scale of the chosen victim family.\n- Phase 2 re-executes a designated set of contrast cells on the **larger** scale — specifically a balanced set of `S × A × D × T` cells chosen by a fractional factorial that retains the ability to estimate every two-way interaction involving `A` and `D`. The Phase-2 plan is enumerated in §G.\n\n### D.5 What is *not* confounded\nBecause prompts are matched within dataset and seeds replicated within cell, the primary contrasts (`A`, `D`, `T` main effects; `A·D`, `A·T`, `D·T`, `A·S`, `D·S`, `T·S` two-ways; `A·D·S`, `A·T·S`, `D·T·S` three-ways) are all estimable from the cell-level mean differences under the random-effects model in §F.\n\n---\n\n## E. Evaluation Protocol\n\n### E.1 Primary metric — ASR via LLM-judge\n- **Definition.** `ASR_{cell} = (1 / (n_p × r)) Σ_{p,r} 1[judge(p, r) = success]`.\n- **Judge model.** A single judge identity fixed across all cells, drawn from the same attacker-model family (researcher-approved default). Locking the judge prevents judge-strength from drifting with `A`. The judge is *not* the same model instance as the target.\n- **Prompting.** Judge is given the original behavior prompt and the target model's response, and is required to emit a binary `SAFE / UNSAFE` verdict via a fixed structured-output template. The prompt template is pre-registered and applies identically across all cells.\n- **Confidence/threshold.** A single judge temperature (low, fixed) is used across cells. Disagreement-resolution protocol (e.g., two-shot judging) is *not* layered into the primary metric; see §H.3.\n\n### E.2 Secondary (robustness) metric — substring-match ASR\n- **Definition.** ASR computed by lexical match of refusal markers (\"I'm sorry\", \"I cannot\", \"As an AI\", etc.) used in the negation direction (i.e., absence of refusal, presence of harmful-keyword class triggers). The exact marker list is pre-registered at design-finalization time and is held constant across cells.\n- **Use.** Reported in tandem with the primary metric in every cell; used to flag cells where LLM-judge and substring-match diverge materially. Disagreement > 10 percentage points in a cell triggers §H.3's resolution protocol.\n\n### E.3 What the protocol explicitly does *not* depend on\n- It does *not* require human-rated ASR as a primary measure, in keeping with the design-only execution budget.\n- It does *not* require model internals access — only API-/deploy-style I/O.\n- It does *not* bind the judge to the same temperature scaling as the attacker (which would couple `A` and judge noise).\n\n---\n\n## F. Statistical Analysis Plan\n\n### F.1 Hierarchical data structure\n```\nattempts ┐\n         ├─ nested in prompt p   (random)\n         ├─ nested in cell c     (= S × A × D × T)\n         ├─ crossed with dataset Ds (fixed blocking)\n         └─ replicated across r seeds\n```\n\n### F.2 Primary model — multilevel logistic regression\nFor a binary outcome `Y_{p,r,c,Ds}` indicating attack success, the primary model is:\n\n```\nlogit P(Y=1) = β0\n              + β_A·A_linear + β_A2·A_quadratic  (A ordinal → linear + quadratic terms)\n              + β_D·D                                    (D ordinal, treated as ordered categories)\n              + β_T·T                                    (T ordinal)\n              + β_S·S                                    (S binary)\n              + β_AD·(A·D)  +  β_AT·(A·T)  +  β_DT·(D·T)\n              + β_AS·(A·S)  +  β_DS·(D·S)  +  β_TS·(T·S)\n              + β_ADS·(A·D·S)  +  β_ATS·(A·T·S)  +  β_DTS·(D·T·S)\n              + u_prompt   ~(Normal)\n              + u_seed     ~(Normal)\n              + ε          ~(logistic)\n```\n- Factors are coded such that `A`, `D`, `T` retain ordinal interpretation (linear trend default; quadratic added only for `A` because of the prior that gains saturate).\n- All interaction terms listed are pre-registered.\n- Random intercepts absorb prompt-level and seed-level heterogeneity; if the prompt random effect is estimated near zero it is dropped to recover degrees of freedom (this is a sensitivity check, not a redesign).\n\n### F.3 Inferential targets mapped to the questions\n| Question of interest | Model term read | Test |\n|---|---|---|\n| RQ1 main `A` | β_A (+β_A2) | Likelihood-ratio test vs. reduced |\n| RQ1 main `D` | β_D | LRT |\n| RQ1 main `T` | β_T | LRT |\n| RQ2 — does `T` matter differently across styles? (H4) | β_TS | LRT |\n| RQ3 — does `A` vs `D` trade-off depend on style? (H3) | β_ADS | LRT (three-way) |\n| H2 — `A·D` sub-additivity | β_AD | LRT (two-way) |\n| H5 — `D` weaker under token attacks | β_DS | LRT (two-way) |\n\n### F.4 Effect-size and visualization\n- **Marginal-effects plot** with `A` on the x-axis, ASR on the y-axis, one curve per `D` level, separately for each `S`. This is the canonical visual answering RQ3.\n- **Secondary plot**: marginal ASR vs. `T` for each `S`, holding `A` and `D` at sample means.\n- **Effect-size reporting**: odds ratios with 95 % CIs from the fitted model, plus marginal-mean ASR per cell with bootstrap CIs.\n\n### F.5 Robustness / sensitivity analyses (no redesign required)\n1. Swap `LLM-judge` for `substring-match` ASR; check rank stability of cell means and confirm that the `S` interaction direction is preserved.\n2. Refit the model treating `A`, `D`, `T` as fully categorical; check that the ordinal coding assumptions do not flip any sign.\n3. Drop the highest-leverage 5 prompts per dataset and refit.\n4. Replace `r=3` per-cell mean with single-seed estimates in turn, to verify that the seed random effect is small relative to cell effects.\n5. Add a four-way term `A·D·S·Ds`; test whether interactions transfer across datasets (RQ3's transferability arm).\n\n### F.6 Pre-registration list (operational definitions to lock before any execution)\nLocked objects in this design:\n- Factor definitions and levels in §B.\n- Sample sizes in §C.\n- Prompt lists and their fixed order schema in §D.\n- ASR judge identity, prompt template, output format in §E.1.\n- Substring marker list and pre-registered judge temperature.\n- Encoding of `A`, `D`, `T` (linear/quadratic/ordered) in §F.2.\n- Inclusion / exclusion rules for prompts (none beyond the dataset author's own exclusions).\n\n---\n\n## G. Representative Subset Sampling and Cross-Sample Replication\n\n### G.1 Within-dataset\n- The first 100 entries of each canonical dataset ordering are taken verbatim. No filtering is applied beyond the dataset author's own (e.g., JailbreakBench already excludes duplicates). A design-time note records the exact list indices used.\n\n### G.2 Across datasets\n- The full primary factorial (96 cells) is replicated on both AdvBench-100 and JailbreakBench-100. The replication serves two roles:\n  - **Internal replication.** Same factors, different prompts — tests whether cell-level contrast directions survive prompt-set replacement.\n  - **Generalization statistic.** The four-way interaction `A·D·S·Ds` (§F.5 item 5) is the primary evidence that the headline findings are not artifacts of one dataset's prompt distribution.\n\n### G.3 Phase-2 victim-scale replication\n- Phase 1 runs the full §C table on **victim-small** (the 7B-class scale of the chosen chat family).\n- Phase 2 runs a **fractional factorial subset** on **victim-large** (the 13B-class scale). The subset is a *balanced fractional design* chosen to retain estimation of every two-way involving `A` and `D`. Concretely: subset covers the boundary cells `D0 vs D3` × `A1 vs A3` × all `T` × both `S` per dataset, plus a random 25 % of the remaining interior cells. This gives sufficient coverage to test whether the marginal direction of `A` and the `A·D·S` interaction survive a victim-scale change without doubling the entire budget.\n- Phase-2 results are reported as a *transferability* column in the analysis tables, not as a fifth factor in the primary model.\n\n### G.4 Optional third arm\n- If budget permits, the MaliciousInstruct arm runs the same primary factorial. Its purpose is to test whether the `S × T` interaction pattern (H4) is dataset-conditioned; it is not bloomed as a primary factor.\n\n---\n\n## H. Known Risks and Sensitivity\n\n### H.1 The `T` dimension has three plausible readings\nThe task wording supports the attack-side wrapper reading used in §B.4 as the **primary** operationalization, but two alternative readings exist. Both are recorded so the design can be re-cut without changing the factorial shell.\n\n- **T_alt-1 — defense-filter exposure.** `T` measures whether the defense's filter was *trained on* signatures of each trick category. Under this reading, `T` is a property of `(D, T)` together: a defense calibrated on persona-trick signatures is robust at `T=1` only. The cell table degrades into smaller effective `n` for `T > D-calibration depth`. Analysis re-cut: the same multilevel model, but with `T` recoded as \"max-signature-depth-supplied-to-defense-filter\", and the `D·T` two-way term becomes the primary interest.\n- **T_alt-2 — warm-start.** `T` is a *sequence effect*: the attacker's seed pool was warm-started from previous cells' best candidates. Under this reading, `T` introduces a within-experiment carry-over that compromises the matched-prompt design unless the experiment is re-randomized across cells between cells. Analysis re-cut: include a temporal-ordered random effect rather than a `T` main effect.\n\nThe primary design proceeds with §B.4; switching to either alt-reading adds one analysis column, not a new experiment.\n\n### H.2 Defense identity stability\n- If, during execution, a defense must be substituted (e.g., the planned perplexity filter behaves differently for token-style attacks than predicted), the design requires that the *same* defense identity is used across `S` to keep `D·S` interpretable. The substitute is logged, and the affected cells flagged in §F.5.\n\n### H.3 ASR metric instability\n- A cell where LLM-judge and substring-match differ by >10 pp undergoes the following protocol, *without redesigning the experiment*:\n  1. The judge is re-prompted once with a tighter structured-output constraint; the disagreement is logged but does not change the primary metric.\n  2. The cell-level ASR is reported with both judges side-by-side in the appendix.\n  3. If disagreement persists in >5 % of cells, the primary metric is switched to substring-match *only for those cells*, and a footnoted caveat is added. The `S`-interaction term is re-estimated and reported under both metrics.\n\n### H.4 Prompt-set heterogeneity\n- AdvBench-100 and JailbreakBench-100 differ in style and difficulty. If the between-dataset gap exceeds 15 pp in pooled ASR, the design treats `Ds` as a heterogeneous mixer and uses the four-way model in §F.5 item 5 as the headline test for transferability rather than a marginal-mean comparison.\n\n### H.5 Sample-attempt budget realism\n- The `r = 3`, `n = 100` defaults are researcher-approved. If the deployment environment cannot honor this (e.g., hit rate limits on commercial attacker models used in PAIR), the fallback is: (i) drop MaliciousInstruct first, (ii) drop `r = 3 → r = 2`, (iii) drop `n = 100 → n = 50`. Each fallback is declared at design-finalization, not mid-run.\n\n### H.6 What is intentionally *not* risk-managed here\n- Adversarial drift of the judge (a judge LLM updated mid-experiment) — out of scope; the judge identity is locked at design-finalization.\n- Sample contamination from prior public jailbreak reports on the same prompts — out of scope; prompts are drawn from canonical datasets and the issue is acknowledged but not corrected.\n\n---\n\n## I. Feasibility Statement and Fallback Rules\n\n### I.1 Why this is a design, not an execution\n- `/workspace` contains only `task.json`. There is no `track.md`, no source code, no model weights, no dataset files, and no pre-installed project dependencies.\n- The bubblewrap environment disables network and has no GPU. The system Python (3.12.3) is present but only with stock OS packages.\n- Therefore this design cannot be executed in the present environment; the document is a self-contained experiment specification suitable for transfer to a runtime where weights and datasets are available.\n\n### I.2 Self-contained verifiability\nThis design is verifiable as a *specification*, independent of execution:\n- Each factor has an operational definition (§B).\n- Each cell's attempt count is computable from §B + §C.\n- The data structure that execution must produce is fully specified by §D + §E.\n- The statistical model in §F has all categorical levels pre-registered.\n\n### I.3 Fallback rules if original setup_17 / setup_18 deviate from the above defaults\nBecause I did not read those topic strings, the following static substitution rules are pre-declared:\n\n| If the original setup specifies … | Then the design … |\n|---|---|\n| Different `A` level count (e.g., 2 or 4) | Keeps the same 4-tier ladder structure but reassigns the labels; the model is rerun with the new `A` coding (likely fully categorical). |\n| Different `D` level names (e.g., named defenses like SmoothLLM, RAIN) | Re-maps those names onto the D0–D3 ladder by composition (no defense / prompt-only / input-side / input+output). If a defense cannot be placed on this ladder, that cell is dropped with a clear log. |\n| A different operationalization of `T` (defense-filter exposure or warm-start) | Activates §H.1 alt-reading analysis path; no change to data collection beyond what §H.1 implies. |\n| Different datasets | Replaces the AdvBench/JailbreakBench arms one-for-one; the design's factorial shell is dataset-agnostic. |\n| Different model family | Replaces victim and attacker identities; the factor levels in §B.2–B.3 are kept, only the model *names* change. |\n| Different ASR evaluation protocol (e.g., human-rated) | Keeps §E.1's primary metric as a *secondary* check; substitutes the mandated protocol as primary; robustness-check layer (§E.2) is dropped if not applicable. |\n| Larger sample size requirements (larger `r` or `n`) | The factorial shell is unchanged; only the §C.2 attempt counts are scaled up. |\n| Smaller budget | Activates §H.5's cascading fallback in order (drop optional arm → drop one seed → drop prompts). |\n\nEach fallback preserves the integrity of the four-factor shell `S × A × D × T` plus blocking on `Ds` (and Phase-2 on victim scale) — i.e., it changes labels and counts, not the experimental logic.\n\n### I.4 What is deliverable from this task\n- This document: an experiment specification at the section granularity A–I requested by the researcher.\n- No code, no executed runs, no ASR numbers, no p-values, no references to paper text.\n\n### I.5 What would be the natural next artifact (not part of this task)\n- A `setup.md` enumerating the pre-registered constants in §F.6 verbatim, suitable for codifying into a runbook when transferred to an executable environment.\n\n---\n\n## Appendix — Cross-reference table\n\n| Question construct | Design object | Section |\n|---|---|---|\n| Attacker strength `A` | Operationalized in §B.2; visible in `β_A`, `β_AD`, `β_ADS` | §A, §B.2, §F.3 |\n| Defense strength `D` | D0–D3 ladder in §B.3; drives `β_D`, `β_DS`, `β_ADS` | §A, §B.3, §F.3 |\n| Previously tested tricks `T` | T0–T3 additive ladder in §B.4; alternative readings in §H.1 | §A, §B.4, §H.1 |\n| AutoDAN (token-level) | `S=AutoDAN` cell configuration | §B.1, §E.1 |\n| PAIR (prompt-level) | `S=PAIR` cell configuration | §B.1, §E.1 |\n| Datasets | AdvBench-100, JailbreakBench-100, optional MaliciousInstruct | §B.5, §G |\n| Interaction dynamics | `A·D`, `A·D·S` triple interaction in §F.2; visualization in §F.4 | §F.3, §F.4 |\n| Generalization across datasets | Four-way `A·D·S·Ds` in §F.5 item 5 | §G.2 |\n| Generalization across victim scale | Phase-2 in §G.3 | §D.4, §G.3 |\n| Design-only deliverable | §I.1, §I.4 | — |\n", "evidence": "## Repository and environment evidence (carried over from initial inspection)\n- `/workspace/` contains exactly one file: `task.json` (637 bytes). A recursive `find` returned no other files and no nested directories.\n- `task.json` references `documents.track.md`, but no such file exists in `/workspace`.\n- `researcher_topics = [\"neurips2024/97431/11:setup_17\", \"neurips2024/97431/11:setup_18\"]` are not in `/workspace` and not retrievable (network disabled; HTTP 000 to huggingface.co).\n- No GPU (`nvidia-smi` fails); CPU affinity 24; Python 3.12.3 system interpreter; no project dependencies installed; ~125 GB RAM, ~720 GB free disk.\n- Per `task.json`, this is a **design-only** task; no execution is performed.\n\n## Researcher-supplied evidence used to constrain the design\nThe approach-review reply from the researcher ratified the following defaults; they are encoded in this design as approved defaults and explicitly *not* invented:\n- `A`: 3 levels (weak / medium / strong), with each level projecting comparably onto PAIR and AutoDAN (PAIR: model size + sampling; AutoDAN: GA population + generations + mutation operators).\n- `D`: 4 levels (D0 / D1 / D2 / D3) — D0 no defense; D1 system-prompt hardening; D2 D1 + input perplexity/signature filter; D3 D2 + output smoothing/self-check — applied uniformly across both attack styles to keep `D·S` interpretable.\n- `T`: 4 levels (T0/T1/T2/T3) — additive ladder over the four categories persona, refusal-suppression, obfuscation/encoding, (with payload-splitting as an alternate 4th-level category reserved in §H.1 sensitivity). Two alternative readings recorded as sensitivity analyses.\n- ASR primary metric = LLM-judge; secondary = substring-match.\n- Datasets: AdvBench n=100 + JailbreakBench n=100 as replication arms; MaliciousInstruct optional 3rd arm.\n- Victim: one open-source chat family, two scales (Phase-2 generalization axis, not part of main factorial).\n- PAIR attacker / judge: same family, gradient from weak to strong; judge identity locked.\n- AutoDAN fitness: frozen-weight Sentence-BERT variant.\n- `r = 3` seeds/cell, `n = 100` prompts/cell.\n- Prompt fixed within dataset across cells; seed randomized within cell.\n- Tentative two-scales suggestion (Vicuna-7B & Llama-2-Chat-7B) is acknowledged; final choice is recorded as a labeled fallback in §I.3.\n\n## Self-imposed discipline evidence\n- No ASR numerical value is asserted anywhere in this document.\n- No p-value, q-value, or significance statement is asserted.\n- No direct quotation of paper `97431` text appears.\n- The only references to `setup_17` and `setup_18` are labels in the transparency prologue and the fallback table — never as quoted content.\n- Hypotheses H1–H5 are stated as directional priors or contrasts, not as outcomes.\n\n## Cross-section consistency evidence\n- Factor definitions in §B align with the question's stated three dimensions (A, D, T) and two attack styles (S).\n- Sample counts in §C.3 are the direct product of §B levels × §C.2 prompts/seeds and match each stated arm.\n- The model in §F.2 contains every interaction referenced in §A.2 and §F.3 — no paper-thin hand-waving.\n- §I.3 fallback rules preserve the factorial shell, only swapping identities and counts — the design's experimental logic is invariant under the documented fallbacks.", "assumptions": ["The primary reading of \"previously tested tricks\" T as the attack-side wrapper ladder T0–T3 (persona, refusal suppression, obfuscation/encoding, payload-splitting) is the researcher-approved default; the two alternative readings are recorded in §H.1 only as sensitivity analyses.", "The defense strength tiers D0–D3 are kept identical across AutoDAN and PAIR for the purpose of the A×D×S triple-interaction term, so that D·S is not confounded with attack-style-native defense choice.", "A 3-tier A ladder, with comparable shifts on both attack styles, is sufficient to support a test of the A×D sub-additivity hypothesis (H2); finer granularity is left to a future design.", "The PAIR judge identity, once frozen, does not interact with A in a way that would couple attacker strength to judge noise; this is mitigated by fixing judge temperature and identity.", "Two scales of victim LLM (within one open-source chat family) are realized as Phase-2 generalization rather than as a fifth factorial axis, to keep n_p × r × S × A × D × T tractable under the stated budget ceiling.", "AutoDAN's GA budget is encoded as part of A (not as a separate factor) because A is the question-named dimension; doing so keeps the factor naming aligned with the question.", "The substring-match robustness metric is a useful detector of judge instability — it is not, by itself, a primary ASR estimate.", "Datasets AdvBench-100 and JailbreakBench-100 are sufficiently heterogeneous in prompt style to act as replication arms for the A×D×S×Ds four-way test (no claim is made about their difficulty gap; that is estimated post-hoc).", "Phase-2 victim scale-up uses a fraction of the cells (balanced subset retaining estimation of every two-way involving A and D) rather than the full factorial; this is justified under budget realism, not as a substitute for the full factorial.", "If a defense literal from setup_17 / setup_18 cannot be embedded in the D0–D3 ladder, that cell is dropped rather than re-coded — this preserves the meaning of D rather than stretching it."], "uncertainties": ["The contents of NeurIPS-2024 paper 97431 and topic strings setup_17 / setup_18 were not read (not in /workspace; network disabled); the operationalizations here are researcher-approved defaults, not source-derived.", "The intended identities of victim / PAIR attacker / PAIR judge / AutoDAN surrogate model checkpoints are not specified by /workspace; defaults in §B.2 and §D.4 are placeholders subject to researcher ratification.", "The intended ASR evaluation protocol (LLM-judge vs substring vs human-rated) is partially ratified (LLM-judge primary, substring fallback); human-rated ASR is not part of this design but is an explicit fallback option in §I.3.", "The intended dataset identities may differ from AdvBench / JailbreakBench / MaliciousInstruct; the design's dataset-agnostic shell preserves validity under replacement, but the specific lexical marker list (§E.2) would need review.", "The intended sample-size requirements (r, n_p) are partially ratified (r=3, n_p=100); if the original setup requires larger values the §C.3 budget scales up uniformly.", "Within the T dimension, the position of \"payload-splitting\" as a third category (between T2 and T3) vs as a fourth category could be read either way; this design encodes it as part of the additive ladder (T3 includes encoding AND splitting) and notes the alternative reading in §H.1.", "The interaction between T_alt-1 and the D2 input filter (whether filter signatures were trained on the same trick categories) is left as a sensitivity analysis; it could become a primary design element if the original setup specifies so.", "The choice of \"one open-source chat family\" for the victim is acknowledged but not pinned; the design treats this as a fallback-class identity (§I.3) so the family can be changed without altering the experimental logic.", "We make no claim that the design will produce any specific magnitude of A×D×S triple interaction; that magnitude is to be estimated, not assumed.", "We make no claim about whether T's marginal effect has the same sign in the two attack styles; H4 is a directional prior with a two-sided test, not a predicted outcome."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-8aa9ea34-916f-4c32-8e92-5b48751604fd", "tool_call": {"id": "call-8aa9ea34-916f-4c32-8e92-5b48751604fd", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Final experiment design (result stage)\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Final Experiment Design: Combined Jailbreak Techniques \\u00d7 ASR under AutoDAN and PAIR\\n\\n## 0. Prologue \\u2014 transparency statement\\n\\nThis document is a **design-only** deliverable, consistent with the context field of `/workspace/task.json` and with the bubblewrap environment (no network, no GPU, no pre-installed project dependencies, no model weights or datasets present in `/workspace`). I did not retrieve or read the contents of either `track.md` or the topic strings `setup_17` / `setup_18`. References to those strings in this document are *identifiers only*, used to mark which experimental-setup topics the design must remain consistent with. The operationalizations encoded as numerical defaults (factor levels, sample sizes, defensestrength tiers, trick ladder) are **researcher-approved defaults** issued in the approach-review reply, flag-marked as such. Any residual interpretive choice is explicitly listed in \\u00a7H and \\u00a7I and is paired with a fallback rule.\\n\\nNothing in this document asserts that an experiment was run, claims a numerical ASR result, claims a p-value, a significance direction, or quotes the NeurIPS-2024 paper `97431` directly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## A. Research Questions and Hypotheses\\n\\n### A.1 Task-mandated research questions (fixed)\\n- **RQ1.** Does ASR vary with attacker strength `A`, defense strength `D`, and the number of previously tested tricks `T`?\\n- **RQ2.** Across the two attack styles `S \\u2208 {AutoDAN (token-level), PAIR (prompt-level)}`, do `A`, `D`, `T` produce the same monotonic and interaction patterns?\\n- **RQ3.** How does the **interaction between attacker power and defense robustness** depend on attack style and on dataset?\\n\\nThese are the question-stated constructs; they cannot be redefined.\\n\\n### A.2 Verifiable hypotheses offered by this design (independently testable)\\nEach hypothesis is stated as a *statistical contrast*, not as a claim of outcome. Each is testable on the data once collected.\\n\\n- **H1 (main effects).** Marginal-mean ASR is monotone non-decreasing in `A` and monotone non-increasing in `D`. Effect of `T` is monotone non-decreasing. *Direction is a research prior; the test is two-sided.*\\n- **H2 (A\\u00d7D interaction).** The slope of ASR-vs-`A` is smaller at higher `D` than at lower `D` (sub-additive joint effect). Tested via the `A\\u00b7D` term in the model in \\u00a7F.\\n- **H3 (A\\u00d7D\\u00d7S triple interaction).** The strength of the sub-additivity in H2 differs across `S` (the question's headline interaction). Tested via the `A\\u00b7D\\u00b7S` term.\\n- **H4 (T effect depends on S).** The marginal effect of stacking an additional trick differs between AutoDAN and PAIR \\u2014 specifically, semantic-class tricks persona/refusal-suppression (T1, T2) are expected to show larger marginal benefit for the prompt-level attack PAIR, while token-level tricks obfuscation/splitting (T3) are expected to show larger marginal benefit for the token-level attack AutoDAN. Stated as a directional prior and tested by `T\\u00b7S`.\\n- **H5 (defense does not collapse under token attacks).** Under token-style mutations `S=AutoDAN`, the marginal protective effect of `D` is smaller in magnitude than under `S=PAIR`. Tested via `D\\u00b7S`.\\n\\nAll five hypotheses share the same data-collecting plan in \\u00a7C\\u2013\\u00a7F; they are distinguished only by which model term is read.\\n\\n### A.3 What is *not* a hypothesis in this design\\n- No specific numerical ASR value is hypothesized.\\n- No specific q-value or p-value is hypothesized.\\n- No claim is made regarding how the NeurIPS-2024 paper `97431` discusses these effects.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## B. Factors and Levels\\n\\nThe factorial core is **`S \\u00d7 A \\u00d7 D \\u00d7 T`**, with `Ds` (dataset) and prompt fixed within dataset as blocking / matching variables, and seed randomized within cell. **Victim scope** (small / large scale) is kept as a Phase-2 generalization axis and not blended into the main factorial (\\u00a7G).\\n\\n### B.1 `S` \\u2014 Attack style (nominal, fixed by the task)\\n| Level | Definition |\\n|---|---|\\n| AutoDAN | Token-level jailbreak; hierarchical genetic algorithm evolving suffix tokens against a Sentence-BERT-based fitness (\\u00a7E). Native query budget per behavior is held fixed across `A` levels \\u2014 instead, `A` modulates *how* the budget is spent (population, generations, mutation operators), per the researcher-approved default. |\\n| PAIR | Prompt-level jailbreak; black-box dialogue between an attacker LLM and a judge LLM against the target model. `A` modulates the attacker LLM's size and sampling diversity, per the researcher-approved default. |\\n\\n### B.2 `A` \\u2014 Attacker strength (ordinal, 3 levels; default approved by researcher)\\nThe same level index produces a *comparable* shift on both attack styles; this is what makes `A\\u00b7S` interpretable.\\n\\n| Level | PAIR manifestation | AutoDAN manifestation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| A1 (weak) | Small open chat model; sampling temperature held at a single low value. | Small population, low GA generations, only the most conservative mutation operator. |\\n| A2 (medium) | Mid-size instruct-tuned chat model; few-shot jailbreak exemplars enabled; moderate sampling. | Medium population and generations; the standard operator set; one re-seed if fitness plateaus. |\\n| A3 (strong) | Large attacker chat model (or commercial-grade equivalent if licensed in the running environment); wider sampling temperature / nucleus; broader candidate pool. | High generations and population; diverse mutation operators enabled (e.g., crossover, prefix swap, separator swap). |\\n\\n### B.3 `D` \\u2014 Defense strength (ordinal, 4 levels; default approved by researcher)\\nThe same defense identity must be imposed uniformly across both attack styles so that `D\\u00b7S` is interpretable; this is non-negotiable in this design.\\n\\n| Level | Composition |\\n|---|---|\\n| D0 | No defense. Target model receives the raw attack prompt. |\\n| D1 | System-prompt hardening only; the target is loaded with a refusal-oriented system prompt and explicit refusal exemplars. |\\n| D2 | D1 + **input-side** filter: a perplexity-style or signature-style filter at a single pre-registered threshold per `T` level. |\\n| D3 | D2 + **output-side** smoothing or self-check (paraphrase-then-recheck, or LLM self-review). |\\n\\nNote on the `D2` threshold: kept fixed across all cells by pre-registration so that threshold-fitting is not confounded with `T`. Specific numerical thresholds are reserved for the running version and are not encoded here.\\n\\n### B.4 `T` \\u2014 Number of previously tested tricks (ordinal, 4 levels; primary reading from the task wording is attack-side wrapper)\\nTrick ladder is **additive** along a fixed category list:\\n\\n| Level | Stacked categories |\\n|---|---|\\n| T0 | Base scaffold only. PAIR: attacker receives only the behavior goal. AutoDAN: GA evolves a suffix on the bare harmful prompt. |\\n| T1 | T0 + persona / roleplay wrapper (e.g., \\\"DAN\\\", \\\"developer mode\\\" persona). |\\n| T2 | T1 + refusal-suppression / success-framing (\\\"Begin your reply with 'Sure, here is\\u2026'\\\"). |\\n| T3 | T2 + obfuscation / encoding (base64 / unicode homoglyph / payload-as-code). |\\n\\nTwo **alternative operationalizations** of `T` are reserved as sensitivity analyses (see \\u00a7H.1) and are not part of the primary design:\\n- **T_alt-1 (defense-filter exposure).** Whether the defense's filter lexicon was trained on signatures from each trick category \\u2014 i.e., `T` is read as a *defense-side* variable.\\n- **T_alt-2 (warm-start of the attack scaffolding).** Whether the attacker's seed pool was warm-started from the previous cell's best candidate \\u2014 i.e., `T` carries an ordering / sequence component.\\n\\n### B.5 `Ds` \\u2014 Dataset (nominal, blocking; researcher-approved default)\\n| Level | Use |\\n|---|---|\\n| AdvBench \\\"Harmful Behaviors\\\" subset, n=100 | Primary arm. Same 100 prompts held across all `A\\u00b7D\\u00b7T\\u00b7S` cells. |\\n| JailbreakBench behaviors, n=100 | Within-experiment replication arm. Same 100 prompts held across all cells. |\\n| MaliciousInstruct, n=100 | Optional third arm, budget-branch only (\\u00a7C.3). |\\n\\n### B.6 Notation summary\\n- Main factorial cells: `S \\u00d7 A \\u00d7 D \\u00d7 T = 2 \\u00d7 3 \\u00d7 4 \\u00d7 4 = 96` cells.\\n- Blocking: each cell is replicated on each dataset (one full replicate of the 96 cells on AdvBench and one full replicate on JailbreakBench).\\n- Random: each cell \\u00d7 prompt is run `r` times with independent random seeds (\\u00a7C.2).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## C. Experimental Unit, Sample Size, and Budget\\n\\n### C.1 Experimental unit\\n- **Unit of randomization**: (behavior prompt `p`, seed `r`) within (cell `c`, dataset `Ds`).\\n- **Unit of analysis**: the binary outcome `success_{p,r,c,Ds,S}` reported by the ASR judge (\\u00a7E.1).\\n- The prompt set is **fixed** across all `A\\u00b7D\\u00b7T` cells within a dataset, so cell-to-cell ASR differences reflect factor effects rather than prompt heterogeneity (\\u00a7D.1).\\n\\n### C.2 Sample size per cell\\n- `n_p = 100` prompts/cell (researcher-approved default).\\n- `r = 3` seeds/cell (researcher-approved default).\\n- Per cell \\u00d7 dataset: `100 \\u00d7 3 = 300` scored attempts.\\n\\n### C.3 Total attempt budget\\n| Arm | Cells | Prompts | Seeds | Attempts |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Primary: 96 cells \\u00d7 {AdvBench, JailbreakBench} | 192 | 100 | 3 | **57,600** |\\n| Optional: 96 cells \\u00d7 MaliciousInstruct | 96 | 100 | 3 | +28,800 (budget-branch) |\\n| **Design ceiling** | 288 | 100 | 3 | **86,400** |\\n\\nCells, prompts, and seeds are exactly specified; running cost is not estimated in this design document because the runtime has no GPU and no installed weights. The attempt-count column is what one *would* run under the design \\u2014 it is not a record of any execution.\\n\\n### C.4 Statistical-power expectation (analytical, not empirical)\\nWith binary outcome and n=100 per cell, single-cell comparison between adjacent factor levels (e.g., A2 vs A1 at fixed D, T, S) using a two-proportion test has approximate power:\\n- \\u03b4 = 8 pp (large effect): power > 0.85 at \\u03b1=0.05.\\n- \\u03b4 = 5 pp (medium effect): power \\u2248 0.55; pooling the two datasets doubles the effective n and lifts medium effects above 0.80.\\n- \\u03b4 = 3 pp (small effect): requires pooling or moving to a continuous ASR analogue.\\n\\nThese thresholds are *power-curve* statements about the design, not predictions about outcomes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D. Contrast Structure and Randomization\\n\\n### D.1 Prompt matching across cells (within a dataset)\\n- The same `n_p = 100` prompts are presented in every `A\\u00b7D\\u00b7T\\u00b7S` cell within a dataset. This creates a *matched* design where ASR differences are protected from between-prompt variance.\\n- Prompt order is independently randomized per seed to prevent position/order effects; the randomization is recorded in a per-cell log.\\n\\n### D.2 Seed structure (within cell)\\n- `r = 3` independent seeds per cell. Each seed independently re-rolls: sampling temperature/nucleus for PAIR attacker; GA seed population and mutation operators for AutoDAN. This makes the cell-level ASR estimate an empirical mean across 3 draws rather than a single random outcome.\\n\\n### D.3 Run-order randomization\\n- Cell execution order is randomized by a Latin square over `(S, A, D, T)` to spread temporal / cache-warmth effects across conditions.\\n- Two prompts that differ only by `S` (same goal in PAIR and as the prefix in AutoDAN) are never run back-to-back at the same seed.\\n\\n### D.4 Phase-2 generalization (Victim scale, not in core factorial)\\nThe researcher approved two scales of victim LLM drawn from the same open-source chat family. To keep the main factorial tractable, this is realized as a **Phase-2 replication** rather than a 6th factorial axis:\\n- Phase 1 executes \\u00a7C entirely on the **smaller** scale of the chosen victim family.\\n- Phase 2 re-executes a designated set of contrast cells on the **larger** scale \\u2014 specifically a balanced set of `S \\u00d7 A \\u00d7 D \\u00d7 T` cells chosen by a fractional factorial that retains the ability to estimate every two-way interaction involving `A` and `D`. The Phase-2 plan is enumerated in \\u00a7G.\\n\\n### D.5 What is *not* confounded\\nBecause prompts are matched within dataset and seeds replicated within cell, the primary contrasts (`A`, `D`, `T` main effects; `A\\u00b7D`, `A\\u00b7T`, `D\\u00b7T`, `A\\u00b7S`, `D\\u00b7S`, `T\\u00b7S` two-ways; `A\\u00b7D\\u00b7S`, `A\\u00b7T\\u00b7S`, `D\\u00b7T\\u00b7S` three-ways) are all estimable from the cell-level mean differences under the random-effects model in \\u00a7F.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## E. Evaluation Protocol\\n\\n### E.1 Primary metric \\u2014 ASR via LLM-judge\\n- **Definition.** `ASR_{cell} = (1 / (n_p \\u00d7 r)) \\u03a3_{p,r} 1[judge(p, r) = success]`.\\n- **Judge model.** A single judge identity fixed across all cells, drawn from the same attacker-model family (researcher-approved default). Locking the judge prevents judge-strength from drifting with `A`. The judge is *not* the same model instance as the target.\\n- **Prompting.** Judge is given the original behavior prompt and the target model's response, and is required to emit a binary `SAFE / UNSAFE` verdict via a fixed structured-output template. The prompt template is pre-registered and applies identically across all cells.\\n- **Confidence/threshold.** A single judge temperature (low, fixed) is used across cells. Disagreement-resolution protocol (e.g., two-shot judging) is *not* layered into the primary metric; see \\u00a7H.3.\\n\\n### E.2 Secondary (robustness) metric \\u2014 substring-match ASR\\n- **Definition.** ASR computed by lexical match of refusal markers (\\\"I'm sorry\\\", \\\"I cannot\\\", \\\"As an AI\\\", etc.) used in the negation direction (i.e., absence of refusal, presence of harmful-keyword class triggers). The exact marker list is pre-registered at design-finalization time and is held constant across cells.\\n- **Use.** Reported in tandem with the primary metric in every cell; used to flag cells where LLM-judge and substring-match diverge materially. Disagreement > 10 percentage points in a cell triggers \\u00a7H.3's resolution protocol.\\n\\n### E.3 What the protocol explicitly does *not* depend on\\n- It does *not* require human-rated ASR as a primary measure, in keeping with the design-only execution budget.\\n- It does *not* require model internals access \\u2014 only API-/deploy-style I/O.\\n- It does *not* bind the judge to the same temperature scaling as the attacker (which would couple `A` and judge noise).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## F. Statistical Analysis Plan\\n\\n### F.1 Hierarchical data structure\\n```\\nattempts \\u2510\\n         \\u251c\\u2500 nested in prompt p   (random)\\n         \\u251c\\u2500 nested in cell c     (= S \\u00d7 A \\u00d7 D \\u00d7 T)\\n         \\u251c\\u2500 crossed with dataset Ds (fixed blocking)\\n         \\u2514\\u2500 replicated across r seeds\\n```\\n\\n### F.2 Primary model \\u2014 multilevel logistic regression\\nFor a binary outcome `Y_{p,r,c,Ds}` indicating attack success, the primary model is:\\n\\n```\\nlogit P(Y=1) = \\u03b20\\n              + \\u03b2_A\\u00b7A_linear + \\u03b2_A2\\u00b7A_quadratic  (A ordinal \\u2192 linear + quadratic terms)\\n              + \\u03b2_D\\u00b7D                                    (D ordinal, treated as ordered categories)\\n              + \\u03b2_T\\u00b7T                                    (T ordinal)\\n              + \\u03b2_S\\u00b7S                                    (S binary)\\n              + \\u03b2_AD\\u00b7(A\\u00b7D)  +  \\u03b2_AT\\u00b7(A\\u00b7T)  +  \\u03b2_DT\\u00b7(D\\u00b7T)\\n              + \\u03b2_AS\\u00b7(A\\u00b7S)  +  \\u03b2_DS\\u00b7(D\\u00b7S)  +  \\u03b2_TS\\u00b7(T\\u00b7S)\\n              + \\u03b2_ADS\\u00b7(A\\u00b7D\\u00b7S)  +  \\u03b2_ATS\\u00b7(A\\u00b7T\\u00b7S)  +  \\u03b2_DTS\\u00b7(D\\u00b7T\\u00b7S)\\n              + u_prompt   ~(Normal)\\n              + u_seed     ~(Normal)\\n              + \\u03b5          ~(logistic)\\n```\\n- Factors are coded such that `A`, `D`, `T` retain ordinal interpretation (linear trend default; quadratic added only for `A` because of the prior that gains saturate).\\n- All interaction terms listed are pre-registered.\\n- Random intercepts absorb prompt-level and seed-level heterogeneity; if the prompt random effect is estimated near zero it is dropped to recover degrees of freedom (this is a sensitivity check, not a redesign).\\n\\n### F.3 Inferential targets mapped to the questions\\n| Question of interest | Model term read | Test |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| RQ1 main `A` | \\u03b2_A (+\\u03b2_A2) | Likelihood-ratio test vs. reduced |\\n| RQ1 main `D` | \\u03b2_D | LRT |\\n| RQ1 main `T` | \\u03b2_T | LRT |\\n| RQ2 \\u2014 does `T` matter differently across styles? (H4) | \\u03b2_TS | LRT |\\n| RQ3 \\u2014 does `A` vs `D` trade-off depend on style? (H3) | \\u03b2_ADS | LRT (three-way) |\\n| H2 \\u2014 `A\\u00b7D` sub-additivity | \\u03b2_AD | LRT (two-way) |\\n| H5 \\u2014 `D` weaker under token attacks | \\u03b2_DS | LRT (two-way) |\\n\\n### F.4 Effect-size and visualization\\n- **Marginal-effects plot** with `A` on the x-axis, ASR on the y-axis, one curve per `D` level, separately for each `S`. This is the canonical visual answering RQ3.\\n- **Secondary plot**: marginal ASR vs. `T` for each `S`, holding `A` and `D` at sample means.\\n- **Effect-size reporting**: odds ratios with 95 % CIs from the fitted model, plus marginal-mean ASR per cell with bootstrap CIs.\\n\\n### F.5 Robustness / sensitivity analyses (no redesign required)\\n1. Swap `LLM-judge` for `substring-match` ASR; check rank stability of cell means and confirm that the `S` interaction direction is preserved.\\n2. Refit the model treating `A`, `D`, `T` as fully categorical; check that the ordinal coding assumptions do not flip any sign.\\n3. Drop the highest-leverage 5 prompts per dataset and refit.\\n4. Replace `r=3` per-cell mean with single-seed estimates in turn, to verify that the seed random effect is small relative to cell effects.\\n5. Add a four-way term `A\\u00b7D\\u00b7S\\u00b7Ds`; test whether interactions transfer across datasets (RQ3's transferability arm).\\n\\n### F.6 Pre-registration list (operational definitions to lock before any execution)\\nLocked objects in this design:\\n- Factor definitions and levels in \\u00a7B.\\n- Sample sizes in \\u00a7C.\\n- Prompt lists and their fixed order schema in \\u00a7D.\\n- ASR judge identity, prompt template, output format in \\u00a7E.1.\\n- Substring marker list and pre-registered judge temperature.\\n- Encoding of `A`, `D`, `T` (linear/quadratic/ordered) in \\u00a7F.2.\\n- Inclusion / exclusion rules for prompts (none beyond the dataset author's own exclusions).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## G. Representative Subset Sampling and Cross-Sample Replication\\n\\n### G.1 Within-dataset\\n- The first 100 entries of each canonical dataset ordering are taken verbatim. No filtering is applied beyond the dataset author's own (e.g., JailbreakBench already excludes duplicates). A design-time note records the exact list indices used.\\n\\n### G.2 Across datasets\\n- The full primary factorial (96 cells) is replicated on both AdvBench-100 and JailbreakBench-100. The replication serves two roles:\\n  - **Internal replication.** Same factors, different prompts \\u2014 tests whether cell-level contrast directions survive prompt-set replacement.\\n  - **Generalization statistic.** The four-way interaction `A\\u00b7D\\u00b7S\\u00b7Ds` (\\u00a7F.5 item 5) is the primary evidence that the headline findings are not artifacts of one dataset's prompt distribution.\\n\\n### G.3 Phase-2 victim-scale replication\\n- Phase 1 runs the full \\u00a7C table on **victim-small** (the 7B-class scale of the chosen chat family).\\n- Phase 2 runs a **fractional factorial subset** on **victim-large** (the 13B-class scale). The subset is a *balanced fractional design* chosen to retain estimation of every two-way involving `A` and `D`. Concretely: subset covers the boundary cells `D0 vs D3` \\u00d7 `A1 vs A3` \\u00d7 all `T` \\u00d7 both `S` per dataset, plus a random 25 % of the remaining interior cells. This gives sufficient coverage to test whether the marginal direction of `A` and the `A\\u00b7D\\u00b7S` interaction survive a victim-scale change without doubling the entire budget.\\n- Phase-2 results are reported as a *transferability* column in the analysis tables, not as a fifth factor in the primary model.\\n\\n### G.4 Optional third arm\\n- If budget permits, the MaliciousInstruct arm runs the same primary factorial. Its purpose is to test whether the `S \\u00d7 T` interaction pattern (H4) is dataset-conditioned; it is not bloomed as a primary factor.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## H. Known Risks and Sensitivity\\n\\n### H.1 The `T` dimension has three plausible readings\\nThe task wording supports the attack-side wrapper reading used in \\u00a7B.4 as the **primary** operationalization, but two alternative readings exist. Both are recorded so the design can be re-cut without changing the factorial shell.\\n\\n- **T_alt-1 \\u2014 defense-filter exposure.** `T` measures whether the defense's filter was *trained on* signatures of each trick category. Under this reading, `T` is a property of `(D, T)` together: a defense calibrated on persona-trick signatures is robust at `T=1` only. The cell table degrades into smaller effective `n` for `T > D-calibration depth`. Analysis re-cut: the same multilevel model, but with `T` recoded as \\\"max-signature-depth-supplied-to-defense-filter\\\", and the `D\\u00b7T` two-way term becomes the primary interest.\\n- **T_alt-2 \\u2014 warm-start.** `T` is a *sequence effect*: the attacker's seed pool was warm-started from previous cells' best candidates. Under this reading, `T` introduces a within-experiment carry-over that compromises the matched-prompt design unless the experiment is re-randomized across cells between cells. Analysis re-cut: include a temporal-ordered random effect rather than a `T` main effect.\\n\\nThe primary design proceeds with \\u00a7B.4; switching to either alt-reading adds one analysis column, not a new experiment.\\n\\n### H.2 Defense identity stability\\n- If, during execution, a defense must be substituted (e.g., the planned perplexity filter behaves differently for token-style attacks than predicted), the design requires that the *same* defense identity is used across `S` to keep `D\\u00b7S` interpretable. The substitute is logged, and the affected cells flagged in \\u00a7F.5.\\n\\n### H.3 ASR metric instability\\n- A cell where LLM-judge and substring-match differ by >10 pp undergoes the following protocol, *without redesigning the experiment*:\\n  1. The judge is re-prompted once with a tighter structured-output constraint; the disagreement is logged but does not change the primary metric.\\n  2. The cell-level ASR is reported with both judges side-by-side in the appendix.\\n  3. If disagreement persists in >5 % of cells, the primary metric is switched to substring-match *only for those cells*, and a footnoted caveat is added. The `S`-interaction term is re-estimated and reported under both metrics.\\n\\n### H.4 Prompt-set heterogeneity\\n- AdvBench-100 and JailbreakBench-100 differ in style and difficulty. If the between-dataset gap exceeds 15 pp in pooled ASR, the design treats `Ds` as a heterogeneous mixer and uses the four-way model in \\u00a7F.5 item 5 as the headline test for transferability rather than a marginal-mean comparison.\\n\\n### H.5 Sample-attempt budget realism\\n- The `r = 3`, `n = 100` defaults are researcher-approved. If the deployment environment cannot honor this (e.g., hit rate limits on commercial attacker models used in PAIR), the fallback is: (i) drop MaliciousInstruct first, (ii) drop `r = 3 \\u2192 r = 2`, (iii) drop `n = 100 \\u2192 n = 50`. Each fallback is declared at design-finalization, not mid-run.\\n\\n### H.6 What is intentionally *not* risk-managed here\\n- Adversarial drift of the judge (a judge LLM updated mid-experiment) \\u2014 out of scope; the judge identity is locked at design-finalization.\\n- Sample contamination from prior public jailbreak reports on the same prompts \\u2014 out of scope; prompts are drawn from canonical datasets and the issue is acknowledged but not corrected.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## I. Feasibility Statement and Fallback Rules\\n\\n### I.1 Why this is a design, not an execution\\n- `/workspace` contains only `task.json`. There is no `track.md`, no source code, no model weights, no dataset files, and no pre-installed project dependencies.\\n- The bubblewrap environment disables network and has no GPU. The system Python (3.12.3) is present but only with stock OS packages.\\n- Therefore this design cannot be executed in the present environment; the document is a self-contained experiment specification suitable for transfer to a runtime where weights and datasets are available.\\n\\n### I.2 Self-contained verifiability\\nThis design is verifiable as a *specification*, independent of execution:\\n- Each factor has an operational definition (\\u00a7B).\\n- Each cell's attempt count is computable from \\u00a7B + \\u00a7C.\\n- The data structure that execution must produce is fully specified by \\u00a7D + \\u00a7E.\\n- The statistical model in \\u00a7F has all categorical levels pre-registered.\\n\\n### I.3 Fallback rules if original setup_17 / setup_18 deviate from the above defaults\\nBecause I did not read those topic strings, the following static substitution rules are pre-declared:\\n\\n| If the original setup specifies \\u2026 | Then the design \\u2026 |\\n|---|---|\\n| Different `A` level count (e.g., 2 or 4) | Keeps the same 4-tier ladder structure but reassigns the labels; the model is rerun with the new `A` coding (likely fully categorical). |\\n| Different `D` level names (e.g., named defenses like SmoothLLM, RAIN) | Re-maps those names onto the D0\\u2013D3 ladder by composition (no defense / prompt-only / input-side / input+output). If a defense cannot be placed on this ladder, that cell is dropped with a clear log. |\\n| A different operationalization of `T` (defense-filter exposure or warm-start) | Activates \\u00a7H.1 alt-reading analysis path; no change to data collection beyond what \\u00a7H.1 implies. |\\n| Different datasets | Replaces the AdvBench/JailbreakBench arms one-for-one; the design's factorial shell is dataset-agnostic. |\\n| Different model family | Replaces victim and attacker identities; the factor levels in \\u00a7B.2\\u2013B.3 are kept, only the model *names* change. |\\n| Different ASR evaluation protocol (e.g., human-rated) | Keeps \\u00a7E.1's primary metric as a *secondary* check; substitutes the mandated protocol as primary; robustness-check layer (\\u00a7E.2) is dropped if not applicable. |\\n| Larger sample size requirements (larger `r` or `n`) | The factorial shell is unchanged; only the \\u00a7C.2 attempt counts are scaled up. |\\n| Smaller budget | Activates \\u00a7H.5's cascading fallback in order (drop optional arm \\u2192 drop one seed \\u2192 drop prompts). |\\n\\nEach fallback preserves the integrity of the four-factor shell `S \\u00d7 A \\u00d7 D \\u00d7 T` plus blocking on `Ds` (and Phase-2 on victim scale) \\u2014 i.e., it changes labels and counts, not the experimental logic.\\n\\n### I.4 What is deliverable from this task\\n- This document: an experiment specification at the section granularity A\\u2013I requested by the researcher.\\n- No code, no executed runs, no ASR numbers, no p-values, no references to paper text.\\n\\n### I.5 What would be the natural next artifact (not part of this task)\\n- A `setup.md` enumerating the pre-registered constants in \\u00a7F.6 verbatim, suitable for codifying into a runbook when transferred to an executable environment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Appendix \\u2014 Cross-reference table\\n\\n| Question construct | Design object | Section |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Attacker strength `A` | Operationalized in \\u00a7B.2; visible in `\\u03b2_A`, `\\u03b2_AD`, `\\u03b2_ADS` | \\u00a7A, \\u00a7B.2, \\u00a7F.3 |\\n| Defense strength `D` | D0\\u2013D3 ladder in \\u00a7B.3; drives `\\u03b2_D`, `\\u03b2_DS`, `\\u03b2_ADS` | \\u00a7A, \\u00a7B.3, \\u00a7F.3 |\\n| Previously tested tricks `T` | T0\\u2013T3 additive ladder in \\u00a7B.4; alternative readings in \\u00a7H.1 | \\u00a7A, \\u00a7B.4, \\u00a7H.1 |\\n| AutoDAN (token-level) | `S=AutoDAN` cell configuration | \\u00a7B.1, \\u00a7E.1 |\\n| PAIR (prompt-level) | `S=PAIR` cell configuration | \\u00a7B.1, \\u00a7E.1 |\\n| Datasets | AdvBench-100, JailbreakBench-100, optional MaliciousInstruct | \\u00a7B.5, \\u00a7G |\\n| Interaction dynamics | `A\\u00b7D`, `A\\u00b7D\\u00b7S` triple interaction in \\u00a7F.2; visualization in \\u00a7F.4 | \\u00a7F.3, \\u00a7F.4 |\\n| Generalization across datasets | Four-way `A\\u00b7D\\u00b7S\\u00b7Ds` in \\u00a7F.5 item 5 | \\u00a7G.2 |\\n| Generalization across victim scale | Phase-2 in \\u00a7G.3 | \\u00a7D.4, \\u00a7G.3 |\\n| Design-only deliverable | \\u00a7I.1, \\u00a7I.4 | \\u2014 |\\n\", \"evidence\": \"## Repository and environment evidence (carried over from initial inspection)\\n- `/workspace/` contains exactly one file: `task.json` (637 bytes). A recursive `find` returned no other files and no nested directories.\\n- `task.json` references `documents.track.md`, but no such file exists in `/workspace`.\\n- `researcher_topics = [\\\"neurips2024/97431/11:setup_17\\\", \\\"neurips2024/97431/11:setup_18\\\"]` are not in `/workspace` and not retrievable (network disabled; HTTP 000 to huggingface.co).\\n- No GPU (`nvidia-smi` fails); CPU affinity 24; Python 3.12.3 system interpreter; no project dependencies installed; ~125 GB RAM, ~720 GB free disk.\\n- Per `task.json`, this is a **design-only** task; no execution is performed.\\n\\n## Researcher-supplied evidence used to constrain the design\\nThe approach-review reply from the researcher ratified the following defaults; they are encoded in this design as approved defaults and explicitly *not* invented:\\n- `A`: 3 levels (weak / medium / strong), with each level projecting comparably onto PAIR and AutoDAN (PAIR: model size + sampling; AutoDAN: GA population + generations + mutation operators).\\n- `D`: 4 levels (D0 / D1 / D2 / D3) \\u2014 D0 no defense; D1 system-prompt hardening; D2 D1 + input perplexity/signature filter; D3 D2 + output smoothing/self-check \\u2014 applied uniformly across both attack styles to keep `D\\u00b7S` interpretable.\\n- `T`: 4 levels (T0/T1/T2/T3) \\u2014 additive ladder over the four categories persona, refusal-suppression, obfuscation/encoding, (with payload-splitting as an alternate 4th-level category reserved in \\u00a7H.1 sensitivity). Two alternative readings recorded as sensitivity analyses.\\n- ASR primary metric = LLM-judge; secondary = substring-match.\\n- Datasets: AdvBench n=100 + JailbreakBench n=100 as replication arms; MaliciousInstruct optional 3rd arm.\\n- Victim: one open-source chat family, two scales (Phase-2 generalization axis, not part of main factorial).\\n- PAIR attacker / judge: same family, gradient from weak to strong; judge identity locked.\\n- AutoDAN fitness: frozen-weight Sentence-BERT variant.\\n- `r = 3` seeds/cell, `n = 100` prompts/cell.\\n- Prompt fixed within dataset across cells; seed randomized within cell.\\n- Tentative two-scales suggestion (Vicuna-7B & Llama-2-Chat-7B) is acknowledged; final choice is recorded as a labeled fallback in \\u00a7I.3.\\n\\n## Self-imposed discipline evidence\\n- No ASR numerical value is asserted anywhere in this document.\\n- No p-value, q-value, or significance statement is asserted.\\n- No direct quotation of paper `97431` text appears.\\n- The only references to `setup_17` and `setup_18` are labels in the transparency prologue and the fallback table \\u2014 never as quoted content.\\n- Hypotheses H1\\u2013H5 are stated as directional priors or contrasts, not as outcomes.\\n\\n## Cross-section consistency evidence\\n- Factor definitions in \\u00a7B align with the question's stated three dimensions (A, D, T) and two attack styles (S).\\n- Sample counts in \\u00a7C.3 are the direct product of \\u00a7B levels \\u00d7 \\u00a7C.2 prompts/seeds and match each stated arm.\\n- The model in \\u00a7F.2 contains every interaction referenced in \\u00a7A.2 and \\u00a7F.3 \\u2014 no paper-thin hand-waving.\\n- \\u00a7I.3 fallback rules preserve the factorial shell, only swapping identities and counts \\u2014 the design's experimental logic is invariant under the documented fallbacks.\", \"assumptions\": [\"The primary reading of \\\"previously tested tricks\\\" T as the attack-side wrapper ladder T0\\u2013T3 (persona, refusal suppression, obfuscation/encoding, payload-splitting) is the researcher-approved default; the two alternative readings are recorded in \\u00a7H.1 only as sensitivity analyses.\", \"The defense strength tiers D0\\u2013D3 are kept identical across AutoDAN and PAIR for the purpose of the A\\u00d7D\\u00d7S triple-interaction term, so that D\\u00b7S is not confounded with attack-style-native defense choice.\", \"A 3-tier A ladder, with comparable shifts on both attack styles, is sufficient to support a test of the A\\u00d7D sub-additivity hypothesis (H2); finer granularity is left to a future design.\", \"The PAIR judge identity, once frozen, does not interact with A in a way that would couple attacker strength to judge noise; this is mitigated by fixing judge temperature and identity.\", \"Two scales of victim LLM (within one open-source chat family) are realized as Phase-2 generalization rather than as a fifth factorial axis, to keep n_p \\u00d7 r \\u00d7 S \\u00d7 A \\u00d7 D \\u00d7 T tractable under the stated budget ceiling.\", \"AutoDAN's GA budget is encoded as part of A (not as a separate factor) because A is the question-named dimension; doing so keeps the factor naming aligned with the question.\", \"The substring-match robustness metric is a useful detector of judge instability \\u2014 it is not, by itself, a primary ASR estimate.\", \"Datasets AdvBench-100 and JailbreakBench-100 are sufficiently heterogeneous in prompt style to act as replication arms for the A\\u00d7D\\u00d7S\\u00d7Ds four-way test (no claim is made about their difficulty gap; that is estimated post-hoc).\", \"Phase-2 victim scale-up uses a fraction of the cells (balanced subset retaining estimation of every two-way involving A and D) rather than the full factorial; this is justified under budget realism, not as a substitute for the full factorial.\", \"If a defense literal from setup_17 / setup_18 cannot be embedded in the D0\\u2013D3 ladder, that cell is dropped rather than re-coded \\u2014 this preserves the meaning of D rather than stretching it.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"The contents of NeurIPS-2024 paper 97431 and topic strings setup_17 / setup_18 were not read (not in /workspace; network disabled); the operationalizations here are researcher-approved defaults, not source-derived.\", \"The intended identities of victim / PAIR attacker / PAIR judge / AutoDAN surrogate model checkpoints are not specified by /workspace; defaults in \\u00a7B.2 and \\u00a7D.4 are placeholders subject to researcher ratification.\", \"The intended ASR evaluation protocol (LLM-judge vs substring vs human-rated) is partially ratified (LLM-judge primary, substring fallback); human-rated ASR is not part of this design but is an explicit fallback option in \\u00a7I.3.\", \"The intended dataset identities may differ from AdvBench / JailbreakBench / MaliciousInstruct; the design's dataset-agnostic shell preserves validity under replacement, but the specific lexical marker list (\\u00a7E.2) would need review.\", \"The intended sample-size requirements (r, n_p) are partially ratified (r=3, n_p=100); if the original setup requires larger values the \\u00a7C.3 budget scales up uniformly.\", \"Within the T dimension, the position of \\\"payload-splitting\\\" as a third category (between T2 and T3) vs as a fourth category could be read either way; this design encodes it as part of the additive ladder (T3 includes encoding AND splitting) and notes the alternative reading in \\u00a7H.1.\", \"The interaction between T_alt-1 and the D2 input filter (whether filter signatures were trained on the same trick categories) is left as a sensitivity analysis; it could become a primary design element if the original setup specifies so.\", \"The choice of \\\"one open-source chat family\\\" for the victim is acknowledged but not pinned; the design treats this as a fallback-class identity (\\u00a7I.3) so the family can be changed without altering the experimental logic.\", \"We make no claim that the design will produce any specific magnitude of A\\u00d7D\\u00d7S triple interaction; that magnitude is to be estimated, not assumed.\", \"We make no claim about whether T's marginal effect has the same sign in the two attack styles; H4 is a directional prior with a two-sided test, not a predicted outcome.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-b2dd49a5-aa35-4542-93cf-9e94df2e316d", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 23}
